From f57de8800c876c40c1c6f436609dfd1f487d2038 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 16:45:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 122/123] dos: patch 0122 -- pump-aware SDL timebase (MODE-2
 ch0 + IRQ-0 perf clock)

WHY DOS NEEDS THIS: SDL/0110's AdLib/GUS music pump reprograms PIT ch0, the
same counter DJGPP uclock() reads -- and uclock() is SDL's ONLY DOS timebase
(SDL_GetTicks / SDL_GetTicksNS / SDL_GetPerformanceCounter / SDL_Delay all
route through it). uclock() assumes it owns ch0 as a full-65536 sweep, so with
the pump active SDL's clock freezes between 55 ms BIOS ticks (with +/-8 ms
local jitter, going BACKWARDS) then jumps ~55 ms. The engine's fixed-timestep
accumulator runs off that staircase, so ~40% of rendered frames repeat an
unchanged world: on gus/adlib the game FEELS like 18 fps while rendering ~28
(GUS-ADLIB-FPS-FINDINGS.md; the "42% deficit" was 1000/54 = the BIOS period).

FIX (SDL-side only): program ch0 in MODE 2 (rate generator, control 0x34)
instead of MODE 3 (0x36). Both fire IRQ-0 once per div counts, so the music
tick rate and the BIOS-INT8 chain are unchanged -- but MODE 2's latched counter
single-sweeps div->1 linearly, so sub-tick phase is readable. The pump ISR
keeps a DPMI-locked period counter (s_isr_count); a main-line reader returns
the monotonic PIT-clock delta since Start (isr_count*div + phase) with a
uclock-style consistency retry around the latch; SDL_systimer.c derives
SDL_GetPerformanceCounter (and SDL_SYS_DelayNS) from it while the pump owns ch0,
rebased at PumpStart (s_pump_perf_base = uclock() snapshot) so the timeline is
continuous with uclock before and after. Both sources share the ~1.193 MHz PIT
frequency, so UCLOCKS_PER_SEC stays the perf frequency.

Killswitch SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_PUMP_TIMEBASE=0 (strict-"0", DEFAULT ON) reverts
ch0 to MODE 3 + uclock -- byte-identical to pre-0122 -- the escape hatch if
MODE-2 latch reads misbehave on some real board (the design's RTC/IRQ-8 fallback
is the next step; see SDL_dosopltimer.h). One-shot boot banner + a PumpStop
ride-along that prints isr_count, expected BIOS chains (isr_count*div/65536) and
actual chains, turning the standing pump clock-skew question into a printed
number on every run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
